<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-1.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-1.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="481" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112245" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-1.jpeg 1383w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-1-300x172.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-1-1024x587.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-1-768x440.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>As I was whining <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2025/04/16/do-lists-of-published-works-exist/">yesterday</a>, it&#8217;s perplexingly difficult to find a <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2025/04/16/do-lists-of-published-works-exist/">(semi-)programmatic way</a> of determining whether an author has written a new book.  Or even manually in some cases.  The best advice is, like, &#8220;Follow them on Goodreads&#8221; or something?</p>
<p>(To recap those two previous posts for those who hate clicking links &#8212; there&#8217;s plenty of databases of published books, but as far as I can tell, there are no databases of &#8220;published works&#8221;.  That is, if the book was published today, would you consider it to be &#8220;a new work by this author&#8221; and not a new edition, a reprint, a translation, or whatever.)</p>
<p>But I wondered whether I could finally find something useful for an LLM to do?  Sure, LLMs are plagiarism machines that use all of the world&#8217;s energy, but what can it do for me?  So I signed up for the Perplexity AI, and an hour later:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-2.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-2.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="440" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112246" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-2.jpeg 1222w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-2-300x157.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-2-1024x537.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-2-768x403.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Yes, I&#8217;m a prompt engineer now.</p>
<p>I&#8217;ve gotta give it to Perplexity &#8212; signing up for the API, adding some money and getting it to spew some data at me was totally painless.  I created a little library called <tt>perplexity.el</tt> (and put it on <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/bookiez.el" data-cached-time="2025-04-17T02:25:14" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/cache-2025-04-17-web-1.webp" onmouseenter="hoverLink(event)">Microsoft Github</a>), but it&#8217;s totally trivial: It just does a <tt>url-retrieve</tt> request with some headers set, and you get JSON back.</p>
<p>So in the screenshot above, it&#8217;s listing all the books by David Sedaris it knows about, and also helpfully what kind of book it is. </p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-3.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-3.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="151" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112247" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-3.jpeg 1148w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-3-300x54.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-3-1024x185.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-3-768x138.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>That&#8217;s OK, and it&#8217;s of course trivial to just use that to list books that are newer than the last book I have from him.  So I guess I could use this to automatically get all new books from the authors I&#8217;m interested in.</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-4.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-4.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="346" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112248" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-4.jpeg 1240w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-4-300x124.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-4-1024x423.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-4-768x317.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>I also did a variation where I asked &#8220;list all the books by this author, but exclude books on this list:&#8221;, and then list all the books I already have by this author.</p>
<p>And as you can see, that, er, kinda changed what it&#8217;s outputting?  Now it&#8217;s including a lots of plays that it didn&#8217;t before?</p>
<p>Typically enough, LLMs give different answers every time I ask, so this isn&#8217;t, you know, data to be relied on in any way.  Sure, it&#8217;d be better if it was actual data instead of hallucinations and LLM navel lint, but it&#8217;s better than nothing.</p>
<p>The response times from Perplexity are all over the place &#8212; sometimes it responds within a couple seconds, and sometimes it takes a minute, but whatchagonnado.</p>
<p>Let&#8217;s test another author:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-7.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-7.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="383" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112256" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-7.jpeg 1379w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-7-300x137.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-7-1024x467.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-7-768x350.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>Amy Hempel.  She&#8217;s awesome.  I hit the <tt>m</tt> command (to list &#8220;missing&#8221; books), and:</p>
<p><a class=cimage href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-8.jpeg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-8.jpeg" alt="" width="840" height="111" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-112257" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-8.jpeg 1061w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-8-300x40.jpeg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-8-1024x136.jpeg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/2025-04-17-8-768x102.jpeg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>All those three are books that exist!  And that I don&#8217;t have.</p>
<p>I tried asking it again, and then it only listed two of these books &#8212; the non-repeatability of the results is annoying, but again, LLMs are toys, and if you get anything useful out of them, that&#8217;s nice, but your expectations can&#8217;t be low enough.</p>
<p>For giggles, let&#8217;s try this a &#8220;list missing books&#8221; on Megan Whalen a few times:</p>
